From: Trijal Saha <97483939+Trijal08@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ffi: take over the vendor's two carrier databases

ModemManager's samsung-sit plugin read the vendor's cfg.db and ims.db
itself: 1046 lines of vendor schema, vendor tag numbers, and the vendor
resolver's four-tier ranking reimplemented in C. That is this project's
knowledge, not a modem manager's, and holding it there is what kept the
plugin from going upstream.

Two crates take it over, next to the codecs that build the frames their
values end up in:

  samsung-carrier-db   cfg.db -> which carrier manifest a SIM gets. The
                       vendor's own four tiers in order: SIM privilege
                       rule, the ranked carrier_info match, an ICCID IIN
                       prefix, the global fallback. Getting this wrong
                       does not fail loudly, it just applies another
                       carrier's rules, so it reproduces the ranking
                       exactly rather than improving on it.

  samsung-ims-config   ims.db -> the CP's per-carrier IMS settings and
                       APN list, handed back as the same
                       SamsungAimsConfigEntry the hidden-menu builder
                       already takes.

Both are exposed through samsung-sit-ffi as opaque handles, because the
entries point at strings the handle owns. SQLite comes from the system
here, as in samsung-ims-config's sibling carrier-settings; a static
consumer of the archive needs it on its own link line, so the pkg-config
file now says Requires.private: sqlite3.

Each resolver was checked against the C it replaces, compiled as an
oracle, over every identity the shipped databases contain -- 2582 for
ims.db and 3964 for cfg.db -- with zero differences in entries, order,
User-Agent or selected manifest. The ported unit tests travel with the
crates.
